1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,597
This is ECE 320, video 22 c, part 3 of the multicore unit: memory consistency.

2
00:00:09,047 --> 00:00:16,599
After this video, you can do four things. One: explain how a store buffer changes what another core sees.

3
00:00:17,049 --> 00:00:22,489
Two: decide if a result is possible under sequential consistency, and prove it.

4
00:00:22,939 --> 00:00:30,001
Three: decide if a result is allowed under IBM 370 or under total store order.

5
00:00:30,451 --> 00:00:34,312
Four: use fences to restore the order that a program needs.

6
00:00:34,762 --> 00:00:43,786
This is the roadmap of the multicore unit. The earlier parts covered threads, the shared memory model, locks, and cache coherence.

7
00:00:44,236 --> 00:00:52,940
The last topic is memory consistency models. A model says in which order other cores see the loads and stores of a core.

8
00:00:53,390 --> 00:01:05,294
A tricky example. All variables start at 0. Thread 1 stores 1 to A, then 1 to flag. Thread 2 waits while flag is 0, then prints A. What can it print?

9
00:01:05,744 --> 00:01:10,757
First, in program order. Thread 1 stores 1 to A, so A becomes 1.

10
00:01:11,207 --> 00:01:15,517
Then it stores 1 to flag. Flag tells thread 2 that A is ready.

11
00:01:15,967 --> 00:01:19,487
Thread 2 reads flag and sees 1, so its loop ends.

12
00:01:19,937 --> 00:01:24,673
Thread 2 prints A. A became 1 before flag did, so it prints 1.

13
00:01:25,123 --> 00:01:35,192
Now let thread 1 reorder its two stores. They use different addresses, so thread 1 alone sees no difference. Flag becomes 1 first.

14
00:01:35,642 --> 00:01:39,269
Thread 2 sees flag equal to 1 and leaves its loop.

15
00:01:39,719 --> 00:01:43,367
It prints A, which is still 0. So it prints 0.

16
00:01:43,817 --> 00:01:50,750
The store to A comes too late. A reorder that is safe on one core changes the result on another core.

17
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:59,413
First, a unicore. One processor runs all threads. The scheduling policy is not defined, and there are no caches.

18
00:01:59,863 --> 00:02:09,762
Each thread keeps its program order. Memory sees one access at a time, so it sees one interleaving: here A 1, A 2, B 1, B 2.

19
00:02:10,212 --> 00:02:18,980
Another run can switch threads at other times and give another interleaving. So the output can change. This is non-determinacy.

20
00:02:19,430 --> 00:02:28,262
Now a multicore. Each core runs one thread in program order. One core accesses memory at a time, and there are no caches.

21
00:02:28,712 --> 00:02:34,963
So memory again sees one interleaving. The possible outputs are the same as on the unicore.

22
00:02:35,413 --> 00:02:41,322
That simple model made two assumptions. One: processors do not cache global data.

23
00:02:41,772 --> 00:02:50,391
For performance, processors must cache global data. This causes the cache coherence problem, which the earlier part solved.

24
00:02:50,841 --> 00:02:53,934
Two: each thread executes in program order.

25
00:02:54,384 --> 00:03:04,965
For performance, we want out-of-order execution. Then a program can mean something else on a multicore, so we must know the memory consistency model.

26
00:03:05,415 --> 00:03:16,274
A unicore reorders only when the control and data dependences allow it. The two stores of thread 1 use different addresses, so core 0 may swap them.

27
00:03:16,724 --> 00:03:27,604
But thread 2 needs that order, and core 1 does not know what core 0 reordered. We need rules that keep programming intuitive and keep out-of-order performance.

28
00:03:28,054 --> 00:03:35,115
Memory consistency uses the cache coherence protocol, but the two terms answer different questions.

29
00:03:35,565 --> 00:03:43,608
Coherence says how other processors see an update to one location. Every processor sees the writes to A in one order.

30
00:03:44,058 --> 00:03:54,042
Consistency says when another processor sees an update. It is about the order of the updates to all locations, and which orders are acceptable.

31
00:03:54,492 --> 00:04:04,583
The consistency model defines correct behavior. It is a contract between the system and the programmer, like the ISA, and software can see it.

32
00:04:05,033 --> 00:04:10,195
The coherence protocol is only a means to an end. Software cannot see it.

33
00:04:10,645 --> 00:04:18,091
So the consistency model restricts the order of loads and stores, not the order of coherence messages.

34
00:04:18,541 --> 00:04:24,301
Recall the store buffer from the caching lecture. It hides the latency of store misses.

35
00:04:24,751 --> 00:04:29,167
On a load miss, the load needs the data, so the pipeline must stall.

36
00:04:29,617 --> 00:04:35,441
On a store miss, no instruction waits for the data. So there is no reason to stall.

37
00:04:35,891 --> 00:04:42,803
The store puts its address and value into the store buffer, here A equals 1, and the processor keeps going.

38
00:04:43,253 --> 00:04:47,733
The store buffer writes the store into the data cache in the background.

39
00:04:48,183 --> 00:04:55,180
A load must search the store buffer and the data cache, because the newest value can still be in the buffer.

40
00:04:55,630 --> 00:05:04,803
The writeback buffer is different: it sits behind the data cache and hides writebacks. On a multicore, the store buffer causes a problem.

41
00:05:05,253 --> 00:05:17,925
Here is the same program on two processors. Processor 0 writes A, then flag. Processor 1 spins until flag is 1, then prints A. Intuition says it prints 1.

42
00:05:18,375 --> 00:05:27,271
Processor 0 executes A equals 1. The store misses the cache, so it waits in the store buffer. Memory A is still 0.

43
00:05:27,721 --> 00:05:35,849
Processor 0 keeps going. flag equals 1 hits the cache and completes at once. Memory flag becomes 1.

44
00:05:36,299 --> 00:05:40,353
Processor 1 reads flag and sees 1, so it leaves its loop.

45
00:05:40,803 --> 00:05:48,504
Processor 1 prints A. Memory A is still 0, because A equals 1 waits in the store buffer. It prints 0.

46
00:05:48,954 --> 00:05:56,037
Later, the store buffer writes A equals 1 to memory. Too late: processor 1 already printed 0.

47
00:05:56,487 --> 00:06:06,855
Coherence says nothing here. It orders the writes to one location, but processor 1 saw the writes to two locations, flag and A, out of order.

48
00:06:07,305 --> 00:06:16,627
The store buffer reordered the writes. Real systems do this. What is allowed is part of the ISA: the memory consistency model.

49
00:06:17,077 --> 00:06:25,547
A memory consistency model is a contract between the software and the memory. Models differ in the reorders that they allow.

50
00:06:25,997 --> 00:06:36,173
Program order is the order of one processor's own accesses, as its program gives it. A 1 comes before B 1, and A 2 before B 2.

51
00:06:36,623 --> 00:06:45,924
Visibility order is the order of all accesses, as the processors observe them. It merges all accesses into one sequence.

52
00:06:46,374 --> 00:06:54,502
The slides write it as a V-order list, here A 1, A 2, B 1, B 2. A model says which V-orders are allowed.

53
00:06:54,952 --> 00:07:05,299
The strongest model is strict consistency. A read of x returns the value of the most recent write to x, no matter how soon the read comes.

54
00:07:05,749 --> 00:07:17,098
Case 1: CPU 1 writes X first, then CPU 2 reads X twice. Can u, v be 1, 1? Yes, with the V-order A 1, B 1, B 2.

55
00:07:17,548 --> 00:07:24,481
Can case 1 give 0, 1? No. That needs B 1 before A 1, but A 1 came first in time.

56
00:07:24,931 --> 00:07:35,043
Case 2: CPU 2 reads X before CPU 1 writes it. Now 0, 1 is correct, with the V-order B 1, A 1, B 2.

57
00:07:35,493 --> 00:07:43,088
Strict consistency needs a global clock to find the most recent write, and writes that happen at once, everywhere.

58
00:07:43,538 --> 00:07:56,125
That fails in distributed shared memory, where a local access is faster than a remote one. With caches, a write must first invalidate the other copies. So it is not practical.

59
00:07:56,575 --> 00:08:03,444
So what do we assume when we write multithreaded programs? The next model: sequential consistency.

60
00:08:03,894 --> 00:08:13,537
Sequential consistency is slightly weaker. It does not fix what a read returns in real time. It says which reads and writes could happen.

61
00:08:13,987 --> 00:08:24,781
Lamport, 1979: the result of any execution is the same as if the operations of all processors ran in some sequential order,

62
00:08:25,231 --> 00:08:30,970
and the operations of each processor appear in this sequence in the order of its program.

63
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:40,423
So two rules. Rule 1: all instructions run in some sequential order. Rule 2: each processor keeps its program order.

64
00:08:40,873 --> 00:08:50,515
The model has one memory, no caches and no store buffers. A switch connects one processor at a time, so memory sees one interleaving.

65
00:08:50,965 --> 00:09:04,597
The slide example. CPU 1 writes X, then reads Y into u. CPU 2 writes Y, then reads X into v. All start at 0. Which u, v are possible under SC?

66
00:09:05,047 --> 00:09:13,751
Build one interleaving, one operation at a time. A 1: CPU 1 stores 1 to X. Memory X becomes 1.

67
00:09:14,201 --> 00:09:19,065
A 2: CPU 2 stores 1 to Y. Memory Y becomes 1.

68
00:09:19,515 --> 00:09:25,553
B 1: CPU 1 loads Y into u. Y is already 1, so u becomes 1.

69
00:09:26,003 --> 00:09:31,485
B 2: CPU 2 loads X into v. X is 1, so v becomes 1.

70
00:09:31,935 --> 00:09:42,239
Each CPU kept its program order, so this interleaving is legal. So 1, 1 is possible, with the V-order A 1, A 2, B 1, B 2.

71
00:09:42,689 --> 00:09:47,767
Start again from 0 with another order: A 2, B 2, A 1, B 1.

72
00:09:48,217 --> 00:09:53,081
A 2: CPU 2 stores 1 to Y. Memory Y becomes 1.

73
00:09:53,531 --> 00:09:59,952
B 2: CPU 2 loads X into v. No one has written X yet, so v gets 0.

74
00:10:00,402 --> 00:10:07,485
A 1: CPU 1 stores 1 to X. Memory X becomes 1, but v already holds 0.

75
00:10:07,935 --> 00:10:13,503
B 1: CPU 1 loads Y into u. Y is 1, so u becomes 1.

76
00:10:13,953 --> 00:10:19,777
So 1, 0 is also possible, with the V-order A 2, B 2, A 1, B 1.

77
00:10:20,227 --> 00:10:28,141
Can u, v be 0, 0? Program order gives two arrows: A 1 before B 1, and A 2 before B 2.

78
00:10:28,591 --> 00:10:35,290
u equals 0 means B 1 read Y before A 2 wrote it. So B 1 comes before A 2.

79
00:10:35,740 --> 00:10:42,183
v equals 0 means B 2 read X before A 1 wrote it. So B 2 comes before A 1.

80
00:10:42,633 --> 00:10:54,921
Follow the arrows: A 1, B 1, A 2, B 2, back to A 1. This is a cycle, so no sequential order obeys all four arrows. 0, 0 is not possible.

81
00:10:55,371 --> 00:11:04,459
The slides give the V-order B 1, B 2, A 1, A 2 for this result. It breaks the program order of both CPUs.

82
00:11:04,909 --> 00:11:14,914
The enumeration agrees. The 6 interleavings give 0, 1 once, 1, 1 four times, and 1, 0 once. Never 0, 0.

83
00:11:15,364 --> 00:11:22,596
Sequential consistency constrains all memory operations. A read after a write stays in order.

84
00:11:23,046 --> 00:11:25,414
A write after a write stays in order.

85
00:11:25,864 --> 00:11:29,192
A read or a write after a read stays in order.

86
00:11:29,642 --> 00:11:40,821
This is simple to reason about. But modern processors reorder all the time for performance, for example with store buffers, where a read passes an earlier write.

87
00:11:41,271 --> 00:11:50,657
The question type: under sequential consistency, is a result possible? Or: list all possible results. This method solves both.

88
00:11:51,107 --> 00:11:56,462
Step 1: label the memory operations, and draw the program-order arrows.

89
00:11:56,912 --> 00:12:06,683
Step 2: for each value that a read returns, draw an arrow. The initial value puts the read before the write. A written value puts it after.

90
00:12:07,133 --> 00:12:13,959
Step 3: no cycle means the result is possible. Follow the arrows to write a V-order as proof.

91
00:12:14,409 --> 00:12:20,809
Step 4: a cycle, or a property that every interleaving keeps, means it is not possible.

92
00:12:21,259 --> 00:12:29,665
To list all results, find the reads that decide the result. Try each combination of their values with steps 2 to 4.

93
00:12:30,115 --> 00:12:44,301
The first example of the slides. CPU 1 writes X, then Y. CPU 2 reads Y into u, then X into v. Is 1, 1 possible? Is 1, 0? Pause the video and try it.

94
00:12:44,751 --> 00:12:48,762
Step 1: label the operations and draw the program order.

95
00:12:49,212 --> 00:12:56,807
For 1, 1: u equals 1 means A 2 read Y after B 1 wrote it. So B 1 comes before A 2.

96
00:12:57,257 --> 00:13:06,985
v equals 1 puts B 2 after A 1. There is no cycle, so 1, 1 is possible, with the V-order A 1, B 1, A 2, B 2.

97
00:13:07,435 --> 00:13:11,893
For 1, 0: u equals 1 again puts B 1 before A 2.

98
00:13:12,343 --> 00:13:18,786
v equals 0 means B 2 read X before A 1 wrote it. So B 2 comes before A 1.

99
00:13:19,236 --> 00:13:26,980
Now the arrows form a cycle: A 1, B 1, A 2, B 2, back to A 1. So 1, 0 is not possible.

100
00:13:27,430 --> 00:13:38,929
The answer slide for 1, 0 is not in the PDF, but the enumeration agrees. If CPU 2 sees the second write, it must also see the first.

101
00:13:39,379 --> 00:13:52,925
Assignment 9, question 1. Three processors share X, which starts at 0. Each has a register R. Under SC, can X end as 4, 5 or 6? Pause the video and try it.

102
00:13:53,375 --> 00:14:03,231
Part a. 4 is 2 plus 2. Use the order of the solution. First all of C: it stores 6, loads 6, doubles it, and stores 12.

103
00:14:03,681 --> 00:14:08,801
Next B 1 to B 3: B loads 12, adds 1, and stores 13.

104
00:14:09,251 --> 00:14:15,651
Next all of A. A 1 overwrites X with 1. Then A loads 1, doubles it, and stores 2.

105
00:14:16,101 --> 00:14:22,715
Last B 4 to B 6: B loads 2, doubles it, and stores 4. So X can end as 4.

106
00:14:23,165 --> 00:14:32,914
Part b. The last store to X is A 4, B 6 or C 4. Each stores R plus R, which is even. So 5 is not possible.

107
00:14:33,364 --> 00:14:41,620
Part c. Can X end as 6? 6 is 3 plus 3. Start again from 0 with the order C, then A, then B.

108
00:14:42,070 --> 00:14:45,313
All of C runs first. X becomes 12.

109
00:14:45,763 --> 00:14:50,371
Then all of A. A 1 overwrites 12 with 1, and A 4 stores 2.

110
00:14:50,821 --> 00:14:55,386
Then B 1 to B 3. B loads 2, adds 1, and stores 3.

111
00:14:55,836 --> 00:15:02,151
Then B 4 to B 6. B loads 3, doubles it, and stores 6. So the answer is yes.

112
00:15:02,601 --> 00:15:14,419
Part d. Every memory operation uses the same address, X. So the operations of a processor depend on each other and stay in order. No new result is possible.

113
00:15:14,869 --> 00:15:20,821
The enumeration confirms it. Every final value is even, and 4 and 6 both appear.

114
00:15:21,271 --> 00:15:37,954
Assignment 9, question 2. Two RISC-V processors. x 1 holds the address of A. A to E start at 0, F is 1, and G is 2. Under SC, list all final values of C, D and E. Pause the video and try it.

115
00:15:38,404 --> 00:15:47,513
Step 1: D gets the value that P 1 loads from B in line 5. E gets the value that P 2 loads from A in line 5.

116
00:15:47,963 --> 00:15:55,643
C gets 1 from P 1 and 2 from P 2, both in line 4. The last of these two stores decides C.

117
00:15:56,093 --> 00:16:06,653
Step 2: D equals 0 puts P 1 line 5 before P 2 line 2. E equals 0 puts P 2 line 5 before P 1 line 2.

118
00:16:07,103 --> 00:16:14,101
With program order, this is a cycle, as in the slide example. So D and E are never both 0.

119
00:16:14,551 --> 00:16:26,775
If D is 0, P 1 line 4 comes before P 2 line 4, so P 2 stores C last: C is 2. In the same way, E equal to 0 gives C equal to 1.

120
00:16:27,225 --> 00:16:36,739
Step 3: write one interleaving for each remaining case. All of P 1, then all of P 2: C, D, E equal 2, 0, 1.

121
00:16:37,189 --> 00:16:42,693
P 1 lines 1 to 4, then P 2, then the rest of P 1: 2, 1, 1.

122
00:16:43,143 --> 00:16:51,250
P 1 lines 1 to 3, then P 2, then the rest of P 1. Now P 1 stores C last: 1, 1, 1.

123
00:16:51,700 --> 00:16:55,689
All of P 2, then all of P 1: 1, 1, 0.

124
00:16:56,139 --> 00:17:09,174
Step 4: every other combination breaks a rule. D and E both 0 is a cycle. D equal to 0 needs C equal to 2, and E equal to 0 needs C equal to 1.

125
00:17:09,624 --> 00:17:17,624
So there are exactly four results. The enumeration of all 924 interleavings gives the same four.

126
00:17:18,074 --> 00:17:33,946
Spring 2020 quiz 2, question 5. A and B are memory. u, v, w and x are registers. All start at 0. Part A: under SC, list the possible values. Pause the video and try it.

127
00:17:34,396 --> 00:17:43,377
Step 1: A 3 loads A after A 1, and only 1 is ever written to A. So u is always 1. B 1 sets v to 1.

128
00:17:43,827 --> 00:17:53,513
w loads B in A 2: 1 if B 2 comes first, else 0. x loads A in B 3: 1 if A 1 comes first, else 0.

129
00:17:53,963 --> 00:18:04,757
Step 2: w and x both 0 needs A 2 before B 2, and B 3 before A 1. With program order, this is a cycle. So it is not possible.

130
00:18:05,207 --> 00:18:13,975
Step 3: the other cases have interleavings. All of CPU A, then CPU B: w is 0 and x is 1.

131
00:18:14,425 --> 00:18:20,015
All of CPU B, then CPU A: w is 1 and x is 0.

132
00:18:20,465 --> 00:18:26,353
A 1, then all of CPU B, then the rest of A: w is 1 and x is 1.

133
00:18:26,803 --> 00:18:32,925
So the results are 1, 1, 0, 1; 1, 1, 1, 0; and 1, 1, 1, 1.

134
00:18:33,375 --> 00:18:43,594
Back to the example where SC forbids 0, 0. Now each core has a store buffer, as real processors do. Can we get 0, 0?

135
00:18:44,044 --> 00:18:51,084
CPU 1 executes X equals 1. The store goes into its store buffer, not into memory.

136
00:18:51,534 --> 00:18:57,465
CPU 2 executes Y equals 1. This store also waits in its buffer.

137
00:18:57,915 --> 00:19:07,216
CPU 1 loads Y. Its buffer has no entry for Y, so it reads memory. Y is still 0, so u becomes 0.

138
00:19:07,666 --> 00:19:16,882
CPU 2 loads X. Its buffer has no entry for X, so it reads memory. X is still 0, so v becomes 0.

139
00:19:17,332 --> 00:19:20,553
Now the store buffers drain. X becomes 1.

140
00:19:21,003 --> 00:19:33,846
Then Y becomes 1. The loads read before the stores reached memory, so u, v is 0, 0: sequentially inconsistent. The V-order is B 1, B 2, A 1, A 2.

141
00:19:34,296 --> 00:19:44,515
SC also needs atomic writes: a write becomes visible to all processors at once. Here CPU 3 waits for B, then prints A.

142
00:19:44,965 --> 00:19:53,029
CPU 1 writes A equals 1 into its own copy, and sends an update to CPU 2 and CPU 3.

143
00:19:53,479 --> 00:19:59,025
CPU 2 gets the update. Its copy of A becomes 1, so it leaves its loop.

144
00:19:59,475 --> 00:20:04,915
CPU 2 writes B equals 1 and sends an update to CPU 3.

145
00:20:05,365 --> 00:20:11,189
CPU 3 gets the update for B before the update for A. So it leaves its loop.

146
00:20:11,639 --> 00:20:15,565
CPU 3 prints its copy of A, which is still 0.

147
00:20:16,015 --> 00:20:29,945
Then the update for A arrives, too late. CPU 2 saw A equal 1 before it wrote B, so CPU 3 must see A equal 1 after B. The cause is the read-others'-write-early optimization.

148
00:20:30,395 --> 00:20:37,926
Sequential consistency limits program-order relaxation. So it forbids three performance optimizations.

149
00:20:38,376 --> 00:20:42,280
First: store buffers, where a read passes an earlier write.

150
00:20:42,730 --> 00:20:47,850
Second: reading another core's write early, as in the three-CPU example.

151
00:20:48,300 --> 00:20:52,183
Third: reading the core's own write early, from its store buffer.

152
00:20:52,633 --> 00:21:01,657
To get the performance back, relax the program order or the atomicity, on each core. The next models relax the write-to-read order.

153
00:21:02,107 --> 00:21:10,448
The first relaxed model is IBM 370. It relaxes only one order: a write followed by a read.

154
00:21:10,898 --> 00:21:21,479
A read can complete before an earlier write to a different address. X equals 1 waits in the buffer, and the read of Y goes to memory. u gets 0.

155
00:21:21,929 --> 00:21:31,913
A read of the same address must wait until all processors have seen the write. So the read of X waits until X equals 1 reaches memory.

156
00:21:32,363 --> 00:21:35,137
Now the read of X returns 1 from memory.

157
00:21:35,587 --> 00:21:43,736
So IBM 370 allows store buffers, but no forwarding: a core cannot read its own write early.

158
00:21:44,186 --> 00:21:53,509
The slides ask: 0, 0 is not SC, but is it legal under IBM 370? Pause the video and try it.

159
00:21:53,959 --> 00:21:58,929
In each CPU, the read goes to a different address than the write before it.

160
00:21:59,379 --> 00:22:13,054
So IBM 370 relaxes both program-order arrows, and the cycle is gone. V-order: B 1, B 2, A 1, A 2, the store-buffer execution from before.

161
00:22:13,504 --> 00:22:20,053
So 0, 0 is legal under IBM 370, but not under SC.

162
00:22:20,503 --> 00:22:26,925
The second model is total store order, TSO. It also relaxes only the write-to-read order.

163
00:22:27,375 --> 00:22:36,036
A read can complete before a write is seen by all processors. CPU 1 stores X equals 1 into its buffer.

164
00:22:36,486 --> 00:22:45,766
CPU 1 reads X. TSO lets a core read its own write early, so the 1 comes from the store buffer. This is forwarding.

165
00:22:46,216 --> 00:22:56,392
CPU 2 reads X. Other processors cannot see the new value until all processors see it. So CPU 2 reads 0 from memory.

166
00:22:56,842 --> 00:23:06,762
Then X equals 1 reaches memory. So TSO is a store buffer with forwarding. IBM 370 is a store buffer without it.

167
00:23:07,212 --> 00:23:21,420
Each CPU writes a variable, reads it back, and reads the other variable. Is 1, 1, 0, 0 legal under SC, IBM 370 and TSO? Pause the video and try it.

168
00:23:21,870 --> 00:23:28,441
w equals 0 puts C 1 before A 2. x equals 0 puts C 2 before A 1.

169
00:23:28,891 --> 00:23:38,384
SC: program order adds A 1, B 1, C 1, and the same in CPU 2. This makes a cycle, so it is not SC.

170
00:23:38,834 --> 00:23:53,447
IBM 370: B 1 reads X, the same address as A 1. So B 1 waits until all processors see A 1, and C 1 comes after B 1. The cycle stays: not legal.

171
00:23:53,897 --> 00:24:05,204
TSO: B 1 reads its own write early from the store buffer, so the arrow from A 1 to B 1 goes away. The same holds in CPU 2. No cycle is left.

172
00:24:05,654 --> 00:24:15,190
Follow the arrows: B 1, B 2, C 1, C 2, A 1, A 2, the V-order of the slides. It is legal under TSO only.

173
00:24:15,640 --> 00:24:24,003
The second question type: is a result allowed under IBM 370 or TSO, but not under SC?

174
00:24:24,453 --> 00:24:30,127
Step 1: check SC first. If SC allows the result, every model allows it.

175
00:24:30,577 --> 00:24:35,484
Step 2: draw the arrows of the result: program order and the read arrows.

176
00:24:35,934 --> 00:24:49,865
Step 3: relax each arrow from a write to a later read. IBM 370: only if the addresses differ. TSO: also for the same address, because the read takes its own write early.

177
00:24:50,315 --> 00:24:57,909
Step 4: no cycle means allowed; write the V-order with the early reads first. A cycle means not allowed.

178
00:24:58,359 --> 00:25:05,079
Part B: find a result that TSO allows but SC does not. Pause the video and try it.

179
00:25:05,529 --> 00:25:14,639
Step 1: part A showed that w and x both 0 is not SC, because of the cycle A 1, A 2, B 2, B 3.

180
00:25:15,089 --> 00:25:26,673
Step 3: A 2 reads B after A 1 writes A, a different address. TSO relaxes this arrow, and also B 2 to B 3. Replay it with store buffers.

181
00:25:27,123 --> 00:25:30,963
A 1 puts A equals 1 into the buffer of CPU A.

182
00:25:31,413 --> 00:25:33,162
B 1 sets v to 1.

183
00:25:33,612 --> 00:25:37,815
B 2 puts B equals 1 into the buffer of CPU B.

184
00:25:38,265 --> 00:25:45,411
A 2 loads B. The buffer of CPU A has no B, so it reads memory: w becomes 0.

185
00:25:45,861 --> 00:25:53,072
B 3 loads A. The buffer of CPU B has no A, so it reads memory: x becomes 0.

186
00:25:53,522 --> 00:26:01,330
A 3 loads A. The buffer of CPU A holds A equals 1, so TSO forwards it: u becomes 1.

187
00:26:01,780 --> 00:26:11,721
A 4 and B 4 only add stores to the buffers. So the result is 1, 1, 0, 0: allowed by TSO, not by SC.

188
00:26:12,171 --> 00:26:23,691
Sometimes a program needs the order, for example between a lock and its data. The fix is a fence, or memory barrier: a special instruction of the ISA.

189
00:26:24,141 --> 00:26:32,056
Put a fence between the store and the load in each CPU. CPU 1 stores X equals 1 into its buffer.

190
00:26:32,506 --> 00:26:36,431
CPU 2 stores Y equals 1 into its buffer.

191
00:26:36,881 --> 00:26:46,119
CPU 1 reaches its fence. A fence stalls until the store buffer is empty, so X equals 1 goes to memory first.

192
00:26:46,569 --> 00:26:56,851
The buffer is empty, so the fence ends, and CPU 1 loads Y. Y still waits in the buffer of CPU 2, so u becomes 0.

193
00:26:57,301 --> 00:27:03,680
CPU 2 reaches its fence. Its buffer drains: Y equals 1 goes to memory.

194
00:27:04,130 --> 00:27:15,565
CPU 2 loads X, which is already 1, so v becomes 1. With fences, the second load always sees the other store, so 0, 0 cannot happen.

195
00:27:16,015 --> 00:27:26,425
In a lock, fences go after acquire and before release. They make locks even slower, so use a synchronization library, and do not write your own.

196
00:27:26,875 --> 00:27:40,934
Dekker's algorithm, from 1965, gives two CPUs mutual exclusion with plain loads and stores. Each CPU sets its flag and enters only if the other flag is false.

197
00:27:41,384 --> 00:27:53,245
Under SC it works: one flag write comes first, so the other CPU sees that flag as true. The enumeration never finds both CPUs in the critical section.

198
00:27:53,695 --> 00:28:03,082
Under IBM 370 it breaks. CPU 0 writes flag 0 equals true, and the store waits in its buffer.

199
00:28:03,532 --> 00:28:08,055
CPU 1 writes flag 1 equals true into its own buffer.

200
00:28:08,505 --> 00:28:20,131
CPU 0 reads flag 1. The address differs from its buffered store, so the read goes to memory and gets false. CPU 0 enters the critical section.

201
00:28:20,581 --> 00:28:29,584
CPU 1 reads flag 0 from memory, also gets false, and enters too. Both CPUs are in the critical section.

202
00:28:30,034 --> 00:28:38,055
The fix is a fence after each flag write. With the fences, the enumeration never finds both CPUs inside.

203
00:28:38,505 --> 00:28:49,876
Summary. A consistency model says in which order other cores see the loads and stores of a core. Coherence is about one location; consistency is about all.

204
00:28:50,326 --> 00:29:00,502
Sequential consistency is one interleaving that keeps each program order. Draw the order arrows: no cycle, possible; a cycle, not possible.

205
00:29:00,952 --> 00:29:10,488
Store buffers relax the write-to-read order. IBM 370 has no forwarding. TSO forwards a core's own writes.

206
00:29:10,938 --> 00:29:18,661
A fence stalls until the store buffer is empty, so it restores the order. This completes the multicore unit.
